# Pulse escalation ladder (dotfiles-9z3o) — the consumer for BOUNCED scheduled ticks.
#
# pulse-retry.sh re-fires a bounced tick only AFTER its 🔔 clears, and skips not_ready
# entirely — so a PERSISTENT block escalates to nobody. This watcher is its sibling for
# the other verb: for each loop named in escalate.conf whose bounce episode has outlived
# its grace, it (1) proves the block by capture-pane and clears a stale 🔔 itself
# (dotfiles-jisc), (2) nudges the seneschal, (3) raises the seneschal, (4) files a P1
# `human:` bead and pushes. LLM-free, deterministic, best-effort per loop.
#
# READ-ONLY on ~/.local/state/harness/pulse-bounces.jsonl; its own state lives beside it
# in pulse-escalate-state.jsonl. It never writes a bounce record.
#
# ⚠️ It never re-fires the blocked loop either (dotfiles-t5fj). pulse-retry.timer owns
# that decision and already acts on the cleared-🔔 condition step (1) creates; a second
# owner is a race, measured live 2026-08-09 at a two-second margin. So this unit and
# pulse-retry.timer are complements, not rivals — install both.
#
# NOT ENABLED until Zig confirms go-live (enable via the .timer). Arming is deliberately
# NOT part of the build — the orchestrator installs it, coordinated with the live drain.
#
# Inspect: journalctl --user -u pulse-escalate.service -n 20
#          ~/.local/state/harness/pulse-escalate.log
[Unit]
Description=Pulse escalation ladder: keep a blocked scheduled seat moving

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/.agents/agents/scheduler/pulse-escalate.sh
# Bound the whole run so a wedged tmux / systemctl / curl can never hang the watcher.
# Comfortably over the injector's own worst case (pulse-inject's 90s readiness ceiling),
# which this can invoke once per run for the seneschal nudge.
TimeoutStartSec=180
StandardOutput=append:%h/.local/state/harness/pulse-escalate.log
StandardError=append:%h/.local/state/harness/pulse-escalate.log
